<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-21.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="002353"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002353" label="[00:23:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: hi</span></div><div class="line" id="002359"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002359" label="[00:23:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no for gcc13</span></div><div class="line" id="002410"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002410" label="[00:24:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but generally it will be needed for your further bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="002432"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002432" label="[00:24:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">glibc needs python if you'll build glibc based system later on</span></div><div class="line" id="002443"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002443" label="[00:24:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or it could be useful to e.g. bootstrap Gentoo</span></div><div class="line" id="002519"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002519" label="[00:25:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think fossy was considering moving it to &quot;optional&quot; extension</span></div><div class="line" id="002541"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002541" label="[00:25:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but we decided that python is fairly central part in most modern distros, so you'll probably need it</span></div><div class="line" id="021224"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021224" label="[02:12:24]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I only use live-bootstrap to bootstrap a cross compiler, and finally a native gcc13 stage3</span></div><div class="line" id="021234"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021234" label="[02:12:34]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; then my native packages go on to build everything else, including python, llvm/clang etc</span></div><div class="line" id="021309"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021309" label="[02:13:09]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; are there any steps in live-bootstrap I can drop that are not strictly required to build a gcc/binutils/musl cross compiler?</span></div><div class="line" id="021517"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021517" label="[02:15:17]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; the only thing I actually need to bootstrap directly from hex0 is x86 gcc13/binutils/musl</span></div><div class="line" id="023221"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023221" label="[02:32:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think python is the only thing that you can drop</span></div><div class="line" id="023301"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023301" label="[02:33:01]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">openssl?</span></div><div class="line" id="023303"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023303" label="[02:33:03]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">curl?</span></div><div class="line" id="023407"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023407" label="[02:34:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm</span></div><div class="line" id="023423"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023423" label="[02:34:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps you can skip curl</span></div><div class="line" id="023429"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023429" label="[02:34:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">probably not opensll</span></div><div class="line" id="023432"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023432" label="[02:34:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but not 100% sure</span></div><div class="line" id="023436"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023436" label="[02:34:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, grub needs python</span></div><div class="line" id="023445"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023445" label="[02:34:45]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I don't need grub</span></div><div class="line" id="023447"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023447" label="[02:34:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, that's why we left it in live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="023448"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023448" label="[02:34:48]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I build that much later</span></div><div class="line" id="023452"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023452" label="[02:34:52]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; after I have native toolchains</span></div><div class="line" id="023522"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023522" label="[02:35:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">curl is fast to build though</span></div><div class="line" id="023534"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023534" label="[02:35:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but yes, python will save you some time</span></div><div class="line" id="023536"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023536" label="[02:35:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you remove it</span></div><div class="line" id="023544"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023544" label="[02:35:44]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; true, but I am wondering if the only reason we build openssl is for curl</span></div><div class="line" id="023550"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023550" label="[02:35:50]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; that would be a time saver</span></div><div class="line" id="023556"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023556" label="[02:35:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, python used it</span></div><div class="line" id="023619"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023619" label="[02:36:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but perhaps nothing else</span></div><div class="line" id="023624"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023624" label="[02:36:24]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; openssl is built just before ca-certificates and curl indicating dependency</span></div><div class="line" id="023632"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023632" label="[02:36:32]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; so ill drop those in this next build and we will see</span></div><div class="line" id="023636"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023636" label="[02:36:36]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; those combined save some time</span></div><div class="line" id="023820"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023820" label="[02:38:20]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; at this point the only thing I am still using from live-bootstrap are most of the pass*.sh files which I call directly individually, and helpers.sh.</span></div><div class="line" id="023843"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023843" label="[02:38:43]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; at this point I am wondering if I should just vendor those</span></div><div class="line" id="023856"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023856" label="[02:38:56]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I assume the LICENCES folder indicates the project is licensed as basically everything, so this is no problem?</span></div><div class="line" id="024017"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#024017" label="[02:40:17]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; then there is no dead code people need to audit for stagex</span></div><div class="line" id="024431"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#024431" label="[02:44:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">LICENSES folder mostly reflects license of various patches that we ship withing livebootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="025158"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025158" label="[02:51:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap itself is GPLv3 I think</span></div><div class="line" id="025221"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025221" label="[02:52:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, each file has a license</span></div><div class="line" id="025323"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025323" label="[02:53:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">patches keep their original license from upstream software</span></div><div class="line" id="025341"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025341" label="[02:53:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we can't change that (and no need to do that anyway)</span></div><div class="line" id="025459"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025459" label="[02:54:59]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Yeah fair. Just wanting to evaluate options. (and open to other ideas too!)</span></div><div class="line" id="025512"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025512" label="[02:55:12]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; our use case has some overlap, but only a little bit at this point from the goals of live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="025545"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025545" label="[02:55:45]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Can punt on that for now. Let's see how small I can -actually- get these steps to see how much work we are really talking here to extract just those bits.</span></div><div class="line" id="025648"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025648" label="[02:56:48]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; flex is needed for bison which is needed for bc, and ed is also only needed for bc, and bc is only needed for the linux kernel headers, which I also don't think I actually need bc for? and I could use busybox bc worst case built from tcc which can build uber fast, and also probably eliminate the need for coreutils and friends</span></div><div class="line" id="030123"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#030123" label="[03:01:23]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I could also likely build busybox very very early. Like, just after the first tcc. Then could probably skip all versions of bash, and coreutils, tar, xz, bzip2</span></div><div class="line" id="030158"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#030158" label="[03:01:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; The actual minimum viable path to x86 gcc13 may actually be pretty short...</span></div><div class="line" id="040720"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040720" label="[04:07:20]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Also there are 16+ build steps for autoconf/automake. Are there blockers to using ACR ? <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/radareorg/acr">https://github.com/radareorg/acr</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="041532"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041532" label="[04:15:32]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Tell me if I am smoking something, but this -seems- viable to me: mes ~&gt; musl ~&gt; tinycc ~&gt; make ~&gt; busybox -&gt; acr ~&gt; intermediate musl/gcc versions ~&gt; gcc13</span></div><div class="line" id="091515"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#091515" label="[09:15:15]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Is acr supposed to be a drop-in replacement?</span></div><div class="line" id="091854"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#091854" label="[09:18:54]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">At least it seems more like yet another alternative way of creating a ./configure script. With the same flags as ones generated with autotools, which most ./configure scripts do.</span></div><div class="line" id="092449"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#092449" label="[09:24:49]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Plus for autotools compatibility you'd probably need to keep m4 (and likely GNU m4), the thing is pretty much a framework after all.</span></div><div class="line" id="134856"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#134856" label="[13:48:56]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though, maybe I run m4 and company to generate the actual final compile commands each project needs</span></div><div class="line" id="134907"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#134907" label="[13:49:07]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">commit those in the pass files directly</span></div><div class="line" id="134916"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#134916" label="[13:49:16]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">should still be easy to review</span></div><div class="line" id="134947"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#134947" label="[13:49:47]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then can drop perl, autotools, autoconf, m4</span></div><div class="line" id="135027"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#135027" label="[13:50:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe?</span></div><div class="line" id="135112"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#135112" label="[13:51:12]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">running in a vacuum with fixed filepaths etc, we don't really need any autodiscovery/autogen magic. All we care about are the final CLI commands to compilers</span></div><div class="line" id="135146"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#135146" label="[13:51:46]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah, pretty close to what <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.sr.ht/~mcf/oasis">https://git.sr.ht/~mcf/oasis</a>  is doing with ditching the upstream buildsystem</span></div><div class="line" id="153503"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#153503" label="[15:35:03]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; lrvick: you can get to GCC13 much faster if you don't try to rebuild all pregenerated files...</span></div><div class="line" id="153525"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#153525" label="[15:35:25]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; As for busybox, it probably won't build with meslibc</span></div><div class="line" id="184016"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184016" label="[18:40:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you might distinguish packages into: crucial development utilities, and optional extensions; then next dimension those packages which need autotools, and those who don't</span></div><div class="line" id="184052"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184052" label="[18:40:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the intersection then is: crucial development utilities shouldn't rely upon autotools early during bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="184115"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184115" label="[18:41:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">several options exist to meet this criteria</span></div><div class="line" id="184206"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184206" label="[18:42:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for example, with the approach of tinycc fully replacing gcc, busybox replacing coreutils, a few autotools troublemakers are avoided</span></div><div class="line" id="184214"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184214" label="[18:42:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">already</span></div><div class="line" id="184241"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184241" label="[18:42:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">binutils are a special case, that oasis-linux coped with by stripping upstream buildsystem</span></div><div class="line" id="184342"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184342" label="[18:43:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and since binutils are necessary for kernel and/or tccboot load an alternative was to rewrite those bootloader.S assembly parts for as86 syntax</span></div><div class="line" id="184450"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184450" label="[18:44:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fiwix and/or linux24 are fully supported by tinycc/as86 then, and with tinycc,as86,busybox (bundled into a minimal-i586-tinycc-linux-musl.iso) the development utility package set should be almost complete</span></div><div class="line" id="184626"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184626" label="[18:46:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sufficiently complete to bootstrap a complete GNU with including gcc/binutils/bison/autotools</span></div><div class="line" id="184742"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184742" label="[18:47:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">besides oasis-linux, aboriginal linux (Rob Landley) demonstrated how such a minimal development host setup can be bundled</span></div><div class="line" id="194014"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#194014" label="[19:40:14]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">One problem I see is that modern GCCs require Autogen, and therefore, Guile</span></div><div class="line" id="194032"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#194032" label="[19:40:32]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Unless there's a replacement for Autogen as well</span></div><div class="line" id="194044"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#194044" label="[19:40:44]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or perhaps a version of Mes that can run Autogen</span></div><div class="line" id="213520"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#213520" label="[21:35:20]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Heh autogen is something I have masked on my gentoo installs</span></div><div class="line" id="213603"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#213603" label="[21:36:03]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Seems to only be needed for tests?</span></div><div class="line" id="213618"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#213618" label="[21:36:18]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and gcc builds?</span></div><div class="line" id="213629"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#213629" label="[21:36:29]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean gcc tests</span></div><div class="line" id="213655"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#213655" label="[21:36:55]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you think modern gcc can actually build without autogen? Should kick tires</span></div><div class="line" id="213808"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#213808" label="[21:38:08]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: I also have to re-generate all pre-generated files for my threat model as well. Next step is to switch to VCS sources directly and not trust ar archives even, to avoid what happened with XZ where vcs and archive did not match, or pregenerated files being compromised</span></div><div class="line" id="214001"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#214001" label="[21:40:01]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I was able to drop python, curl, openssl, and everything after gcc13 without issue though</span></div><div class="line" id="214001"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#214001" label="[21:40:01]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so that saved some compile time</span></div><div class="line" id="214030"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#214030" label="[21:40:30]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">As I said, I don't have autogen on my gentoo machines, yet I do have gcc.</span></div><div class="line" id="214030"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#214030" label="[21:40:30]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And looking in toolchain.eclass autogen is only pulled in when tests are enabled.</span></div><div class="line" id="214058"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#214058" label="[21:40:58]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, I wonder why we need autogen for GCC then</span></div><div class="line" id="214119"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#214119" label="[21:41:19]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe in GCC 10, autogen is tested by configure even if tests are disabled?</span></div><div class="line" id="215236"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215236" label="[21:52:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: it's not tests but the build system that needs it</span></div><div class="line" id="215251"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215251" label="[21:52:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gentoo does not rebuild all the build system from source</span></div><div class="line" id="215305"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215305" label="[21:53:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but uses some files that maintainers prebuilt and added to release tarball</span></div><div class="line" id="215319"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215319" label="[21:53:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in particular I think Makefile.in</span></div><div class="line" id="215412"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215412" label="[21:54:12]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you try to build GCC from git repo, you would notice missing autogen much quicker</span></div><div class="line" id="215451"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215451" label="[21:54:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, they actually comitted that Makefile.in to git repo...</span></div><div class="line" id="215456"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215456" label="[21:54:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://gcc.gnu.org/git/?p=gcc.git;a=blob;f=Makefile.in;h=966d6045496026cbc3bc7afd7c2652d0e36b8367;hb=HEAD">https://gcc.gnu.org/git/?p=gcc.git;a=blob;f=Makefile.in;h=966d6045496026cbc3bc7afd7c2652d0e36b8367;hb=HEAD</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="215511"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215511" label="[21:55:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though it is clearly generated (and that is also indicated by a comment at the top of the file)</span></div><div class="line" id="215519"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215519" label="[21:55:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Makefile.in is generated from Makefile.tpl by 'autogen Makefile.def'.</span></div><div class="line" id="215718"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215718" label="[21:57:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Makefile.in is 68973 lines long way too much to read</span></div><div class="line" id="215807"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#215807" label="[21:58:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whereas Makefile.def + Makefile.tpl are 758 + 2165 lines. Far more reasonable...</span></div><div class="line" id="215912"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#215912" label="[21:59:12]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah yeah committed to git is why I wasn't seeing it, because there is git-based versions for gcc in gentoo.</span></div><div class="line" id="220218"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220218" label="[22:02:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's mostly just used as templating engine</span></div><div class="line" id="220241"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220241" label="[22:02:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so autogen is perhaps overkill</span></div><div class="line" id="220244"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220244" label="[22:02:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that's what GCC uses</span></div><div class="line" id="222118"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#222118" label="[22:21:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">seems up until gcc-4.7.4 calling autoreconf -if doesn't need guile; not sure if this re-generates all files or if i missed anything else</span></div><div class="line" id="222247"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#222247" label="[22:22:47]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with gcc-4.7 i build gcc-6.x (for aarch64 cross-target, c++ etc. support for example), and guile wasn't asked for with autoreconf</span></div><div class="line" id="222337"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#222337" label="[22:23:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i've explicitely added autoreconf -if to toolchain.eclass and all ebuilds, guile wasn't needed anywhere</span></div><div class="line" id="222353"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#222353" label="[22:23:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">FEATURES=-test</span></div><div class="line" id="223006"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223006" label="[22:30:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I see, gcc-4.7 too uses a pregenerated Makefile.in, regardless of autoreconf</span></div><div class="line" id="224046"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224046" label="[22:40:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">didn't bother yet with autogen/guile support for the tinycc system profile; live-bootstrap must have arrived at autogen/guile somehow _before_ compiling gcc with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="225024"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225024" label="[22:50:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">parts.rst &quot;Luckily, building GCC without top-level ``Makefile`` is fairly easy.&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="231249"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231249" label="[23:12:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: autoreconf doesn't regenerate everything</span></div><div class="line" id="231253"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231253" label="[23:12:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that file is not even autotools</span></div><div class="line" id="231324"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231324" label="[23:13:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's Makefile.def autogen definition + Makefile.tpl template file -&gt; Makefile.in file</span></div><div class="line" id="231339"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231339" label="[23:13:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that autoconf later processes into Makefile</span></div><div class="line" id="231353"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231353" label="[23:13:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but those early source file (.def and .tpl) have nothing to do with autotools</span></div><div class="line" id="231402"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231402" label="[23:14:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hence autoreconf wouldn't know that it needs to regenerate themn</span></div><div class="line" id="231427"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231427" label="[23:14:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but yes, GCC and binutils is not really one project</span></div><div class="line" id="231434"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231434" label="[23:14:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is a collection of smaller subprojects</span></div><div class="line" id="231437"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231437" label="[23:14:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">smaller libraries and tools</span></div><div class="line" id="231455"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231455" label="[23:14:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you could indeed run individual autotools in subdirectories</span></div><div class="line" id="231503"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231503" label="[23:15:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though it's somewhat hacky but it mostly works</span></div><br /></div></body></html>